= March 2025 — Where does Lewica draw the border line?

Prime Minister **[+ prime_minister +]** asks the Sejm for authority to suspend
asylum applications temporarily at the Belarusian border; President **[+
president_name +]** will decide whether the bill becomes law. The government
argues that Minsk is turning applicants into instruments of pressure. Refugee
lawyers, medics and families seeking protection ask who remains able to reach a
procedure when the gate is closed.

Lewica enters the roll call with **[+ border_doctrine +]** as its operational
record and **[+ asylum_position_2025 +]** as its coalition promise. Border
guards want rules they can apply; Razem and much of our own movement expect a
rights line that survives office. We may reverse ourselves, but nobody in the
chamber will mistake the reversal for continuity.

- @asylum_vote_no
- @asylum_safeguards
- @asylum_support

@asylum_vote_no
title: Vote no and accept the parliamentary consequences
subtitle: Keep the rights doctrine intact and open a breach with the governing majority.
on-arrival: asylum_position_2025 = "Voted against asylum suspension"; border_rights_standard += 5; progressive_credibility += 4; ko_relation -= 5 if government_party = "ko"; pis_relation -= 5 if government_party = "pis"; psl_relation -= 2; government_coalition_dissent += 5 if left_in_government = 1; razem_cooperation += 4; public_trust -= 2; border_permission_pressure -= 4; news_headline = "Lewica records a principled vote against suspension"

Lewica's deputies vote no beside Razem and a small rights minority. Lawyers and
humanitarian groups receive an opposition they can quote in court; coalition
whips receive proof that our border doctrine has a limit. Security-minded
voters hear dissent while the government is asking for unity.

- @poland_event_queue: Record Lewica's no vote

@asylum_safeguards
