    " joins national defence to a capable civilian state";
!}

The answer refuses the choice the moderator offered. Deterrence is described as
a whole system — shelters, hospitals with power, a border service that is
staffed, conscripts who are trained rather than photographed — and the
brigades are placed inside it rather than opposite it.

**[+ pres_2025_right_surname +]** replies that this is a civilian's idea of
defence. It lands with
the audience that has been asked to pay for the last three procurement
announcements without ever being told what happens to their own town.

- @pres25_debate_economy: The moderators turn to taxes and housing

@pres25_security_law
title: [+ pres_2025_security_law_title +]
subtitle: A constitutionally precise answer with a lower emotional ceiling.
on-arrival: pres_2025_debate_score += 2.4; pres_2025_debate_security_line = pres_2025_security_law_title; constitutional_restraint += 3; ko_relation += 1; pres_2025_debate_round = 2; public_mood_pending_rule_of_law_support += 7; public_mood_pending_national_security_support += 4; news_headline = "The Left defines the limits as well as the powers of the presidency"

The answer is exact: the President commands, but does not deploy alone; signs
ratifications, but does not invent obligations; speaks for Poland abroad within
what the Sejm has voted. It is the answer a lawyer would give, and the
constitutionalists in every camp write it down.

It is also the answer nobody repeats in a bar. The studio moves on without a
clip, and KO's spokesman is quietly grateful that the Left has just made his
own candidate's position respectable.

- @pres25_debate_economy: The moderators turn to taxes and housing

