title: [+ pres_2025_security_hawk_title +]
subtitle: Chase the right's frame; gains a hard headline and surrenders fiscal and social ground.
on-arrival: pres_2025_debate_score += 1.1; pres_2025_debate_security_line = pres_2025_security_hawk_title; military_trust += 3; fiscal_stress += 2; labor_credibility -= 2; far_right_agenda += 2; pres_2025_debate_round = 2; public_mood_pending_national_security_support += 9; public_mood_pending_refugee_solidarity_support += -6; news_headline = "The Left tries to outbid the right on its own security frame"

The bid is made on the right's own ground and on the right's own terms: more
than they promised, sooner than they promised. The generals in the studio
audience approve. So does the applause line.

The trouble arrives four minutes later, when the economy block opens and the
same nominee has to explain which budget the extra brigades came out of.
**[+ pres_2025_right_surname +]** has been outbid on a question the PiS-backed
candidate was never going to lose, and the Left
has spent its fiscal argument to do it.

- @pres25_debate_economy: The moderators turn to taxes and housing

@pres25_debate_economy
title: Debate II — taxes, housing and public services
new-page: true
on-arrival: public_mood_pending_social_spending_salience += 12

**[+ pres_2025_konf_surname +]** says the established parties tax without a
purpose and spend without a national plan.
**[+ pres_2025_right_surname +]** promises protection without a bill; **[+
pres_2025_ko_surname +]** lists investments and asks voters to trust the
governing camp to finish them. The Left has one minute to make its economic
conflict intelligible outside a party programme.

- @pres25_economy_signature
- @pres25_economy_mentzen
- @pres25_economy_centre

@pres25_economy_signature
title: [+ pres_2025_economy_signature_title +]
subtitle: Define prosperity in the nominee's strongest voice and keep ownership of the answer.
on-arrival: {!
const scores = {
    "Magdalena Biejat": 3.1,
    "Agnieszka Dziemianowicz-Bąk": 3.2,
    "Adrian Zandberg": 3.3
};
Q.pres_2025_debate_score += scores[Q.pres_2025_left_candidate] || 3;
Q.pres_2025_debate_economy_line = Q.pres_2025_economy_signature_title;
Q.labor_credibility += 3;
Q.household_policy_pressure += 2;
Q.pres_2025_debate_round = 3;
Q.news_headline = Q.pres_2025_left_candidate +
    " forces the economic debate onto the cost of ordinary life";
!}

The minute is spent on three numbers: what a room costs in a city where a
nurse works, what an hour of care costs a family that has no one at home, and
what is left of a salary once both are paid. No tax rate is mentioned until the
last ten seconds, when it arrives as an answer rather than a subject.

[+ pres_2025_konf_surname +] is left arguing against the price of housing as though it were a
government policy he opposes, which for a moment it sounds like. The
after-broadcast panels lead on the figures rather than on the exchange, and the
figures are ours.

- @pres25_debate_rights: The third block joins rights to presidential power

@pres25_economy_mentzen
title: [+ pres_2025_economy_mentzen_title +]
subtitle: A direct counterattack can travel further than the complete answer.
