= 23 July 2025 — From viral act to a court file

After the European Parliament waives immunity and <span class="party-korona"><b>Grzegorz Braun</b></span> appears
for questioning, the Warsaw Regional Prosecutor's Office sends seven alleged
offences from 2022–23 to the district court. One count concerns his attack on
the Hanukkah menorah in the Sejm; the others concern separate alleged assaults,
coercion, insults and property damage. The April Oleśnica hospital investigation
is not among them.

Witnesses and people named in the files need security and independent advice.
Prosecutors need room to prove each allegation, and the court—not Lewica—owns
the verdict. Our accumulated evidence work can strengthen that process; one
careless political frame can also give Braun the single persecution story he
wants seven distinct counts to become.

- @braun_indictment_calendar
- @braun_indictment_victims
- @braun_indictment_front

@braun_indictment_calendar
title: Publish the charges, defences and hearing calendar count by count
subtitle: Let the public follow seven separate allegations instead of one persecution myth.
on-arrival: braun_indictment_response = "Count-by-count public procedure"; braun_legal_preparation += 3; prosecution_oversight_capacity += 2; constitutional_restraint += 3; braun_martyrdom -= 2; public_trust += 2; news_headline = "A count-by-count calendar denies both impunity and collective guilt"

The public file lists each charge, Braun's answer and the next hearing without
announcing a verdict. Journalists can follow the court rather than a party
summary; Braun must defend seven records instead of one useful myth about a
trial of his beliefs.

- @poland_event_queue: File the count-by-count calendar

